# Rego Policy File — `.rego` Schema
#
# Rego is Open Policy Agent's policy language. This file describes the
# structure and conventions used in `agent.rego` so any LLM implementer
# can read and modify it without prior Rego knowledge.

## File Extension
`.rego` — plain text, evaluated by the OPA engine embedded in the
`opa-docker-authz` Docker plugin.

## Syntax Overview

```
package <name>              # Every file declares a package.
                            # The plugin expects "docker.authz".

import future.keywords.in    # Enables the `in` operator (membership check).

default <rule> := <value>    # Default value when no rule matches.
                            # The plugin reads `allow` — default `false` = deny.

<rule> if { <conditions> }   # A rule body. If all conditions are true,
                            # the rule assigns `true` to itself.
                            # Multiple rules with the same name = OR logic:
                            # `allow if { A }` and `allow if { B }` means
                            # "allow when A OR B".

<rule> if {                  # Multiple conditions are ANDed.
    condition1
    condition2
}
```

## Built-in Functions Used in This Policy

| Function | Purpose | Example |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `contains(str, substr)` | String contains check | `contains(input.PathPlain, "/build")` |
| `startswith(str, prefix)` | String prefix check | `startswith(name, "/buildx_buildkit_")` |
| `endswith(str, suffix)` | String suffix check | `endswith(input.PathPlain, "/start")` |
| `object.get(obj, [keys], default)` | Safe key access — returns default if key missing | `object.get(body, ["Labels"], {})` |
| `sprintf(format, [args])` | String formatting | `sprintf("/%s", [action])` |

## Input Schema (Provided by the Plugin)

Every Docker API request produces this JSON, which OPA evaluates as `input`:

```json
{
  "User": "sandbox-agent",       // TLS certificate CN, or "" for unix socket
  "Method": "GET",               // HTTP method
  "Path": "/v1.47/containers/json",
  "PathPlain": "/containers/json?all=true",   // Path without API version
  "Headers": { "X-My-Header": ["true"] },
  "Body": {
    "Labels": { "key": "value" },
    "HostConfig": {
      "Binds": ["/src:/dst"],
      "Mounts": [{"Source": "/src", "Target": "/dst", "Type": "bind"}]
    },
    "Name": "/container-name"
  }
}
```

## Output

The policy sets `allow` to `true` or `false`. The `opa-docker-authz` plugin
reads this value. If `allow` is undefined or `false`, the plugin returns
`403 Forbidden: authorization denied by plugin opa-docker-authz`.

## Evaluation Model

- Every rule is evaluated from every request — there is no caching.
- If `allow` is not set by any rule, it defaults to `false` (the `default allow := false` declaration).
- Multiple `allow if { ... }` rules are OR-ed: the request is allowed if ANY rule's conditions are true.
- Multiple conditions inside one rule are AND-ed: all must be true.

## Idempotency

Rego evaluation is pure (no side effects) and stateless. The same input always
produces the same output. Policy files are read at plugin-enable time; changing
a `.rego` file requires a plugin bounce (disable + enable) to take effect.

